d":"Tartan","title":"Tartan*"}],"dictionaryWords":[{"evidence":[{"matchingRule":"reviewed_qualified_headword","phrase":"Isaiah","senseEvidence":{"basis":"source_verse_citation","originalCitations":[{"anchorIndex":6,"href":"?bref=Isa.20.2-6","rawReference":"Isa.20.2-6"}],"ruleId":"IsaiahPerson::Isaiah::source_verse_citation","sourceHeadwordId":"IsaiahPerson"},"text":"the LORD had already spoken through Isaiah son of Amoz, saying, “Go, remove the sackcloth from your waist and the sandals from your feet.” And Isaiah did so, walking around naked and barefoot.","verse":2},{"matchingRule":"reviewed_qualified_headword","phrase":"Isaiah","senseEvidence":{"basis":"source_verse_citation","originalCitations":[{"anchorIndex":6,"href":"?bref=Isa.20.2-6","rawReference":"Isa.20.2-6"}],"ruleId":"IsaiahPerson::Isaiah::source_verse_citation","sourceHeadwordId":"IsaiahPerson"},"text":"Then the LORD said, “Just as My servant Isaiah has gone naked and barefoot for three years as a sign and omen against Egypt and Cush,","verse":3}],"id":"IsaiahPerson","label":"Isaiah","matchReviews":[{"ruleId":"IsaiahPerson::Isaiah::source_verse_citation","scope":"source_verse_citation","senseReview":"Original title explicitly labels the subject Person. This is a reviewed person headword, never a stripped book title; the original person article must cite the exact occurrence verse. Other people sharing the name are retained within the original multi-sense person article.","sourceHeadwordId":"IsaiahPerson","term":"Isaiah"}],"matchingRule":"reviewed_qualified_headword","matchingRules":["reviewed_qualified_headword"],"sourceProvenanceId":"IsaiahPerson","sourceTitle":"Isaiah (Person)"},{"evidence":[{"matchingRule":"reviewed_exact_headword","phrase":"LORD","senseEvidence":{"basis":"reviewed_capitalized_divine_title","originalCitations":[],"ruleId":"Lord::LORD::reviewed_capitalized_divine_title","sourceHeadwordId":"Lord"},"text":"the LORD had already spoken through Isaiah son of Amoz, saying, “Go, remove the sackcloth from your waist and the sandals from your feet.” And Isaiah did so, walking around naked and barefoot.","verse":2},{"matchingRule":"reviewed_exact_headword","phrase":"LORD","senseEvidence":{"basis":"reviewed_capitalized_divine_title","originalCitations":[],"ruleId":"Lord::LORD::reviewed_capitalized_divine_title","sourceHeadwordId":"Lord"},"text":"Then the LORD said, “Just as My servant Isaiah has gone naked and barefoot for three years as a sign and omen against Egypt and Cush,","verse":3}],"id":"Lord","label":"LORD","matchReviews":[{"ruleId":"Lord::LORD::reviewed_capitalized_divine_title","scope":"reviewed_capitalized_divine_title","senseReview":"Only literal uppercase LORD is admitted globally. Source Lord article explicitly covers God as Lord; lowercase human lord and sentence-title Lord are not inferred from this rule.","sourceHeadwordId":"Lord","term":"LORD"}],"matchingRule":"reviewed_exact_headword","matchingRules":["reviewed_exact_headword"],"sourceProvenanceId":"Lord","sourceTitle":"Lord"}],"format":"bsb-study-chapter-v2","notes":[{"chapterAssociationStatus":"canonical_reference","content":[{"c":["Isaiah"],"sourceClass":"profile-title","t":"h"},{"c":["Isaiah was a Judean prophet during the reigns of Uzziah, Jotham, Ahaz, and Hezekiah. He was the son of Amoz (",{"href":"?bref=Isa.1.1","r":"Isa.1.1","rawR":"Isa.1.1","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Isa.1.1","value":"Isa.1.1"}],"t":"ref","v":"Isa 1:1"},") and was possibly related to King Amaziah. He lived in Jerusalem, was well educated, and had deep insight into human nature. He had a long ministry (about 740~685 BC); as Judah’s political and religious counselor, he had access to kings and was apparently the court historian (",{"href":"?bref=2Chr.26.22","r":"2Chr.26.22","rawR":"2Chr.26.22","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"2Chr.26.22","value":"2Chr.26.22"}],"t":"ref","v":"2 Chr 26:22"},"; ",{"href":"?bref=2Chr.32.32","r":"2Chr.32.32","rawR":"2Chr.32.32","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"2Chr.32.32","value":"2Chr.32.32"}],"t":"ref","v":"32:32"},"). Isaiah’s wife was called a “prophetess” (see Study Note on ",{"href":"?bref=Isa.8.3","r":"Isa.8.3","rawR":"Isa.8.3","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Isa.8.3","value":"Isa.8.3"}],"t":"ref","v":"Isa 8:3"},"); their sons were Shear-jashub (",{"href":"?bref=Isa.7.3","r":"Isa.7.3","rawR":"Isa.7.3","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Isa.7.3","value":"Isa.7.3"}],"t":"ref","v":"7:3"},") and Maher-shalal-hash-baz (",{"href":"?bref=Isa.8.3","r":"Isa.8.3","rawR":"Isa.8.3","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Isa.8.3","value":"Isa.8.3"}],"t":"ref","v":"8:3"},")."],"sourceClass":"profile-body-fl","t":"p"},{"c":["Isaiah opposed social and political evil at all levels. He censured fortune-tellers. He rebuked kings for their willfulness and indifference. He denounced wealthy, influential people who ignored their responsibilities. And he exhorted everyone to obey God’s covenant. Isaiah opposed Canaanite idolatry and insincere religious observances (",{"href":"?bref=Isa.1.10-17","r":"Isa.1.10-17","rawR":"Isa.1.10-17","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Isa.1.10-17","value":"Isa.1.10-17"}],"t":"ref","v":"1:10-17"},"; ",{"href":"?bref=Isa.29.13","r":"Isa.29.13","rawR":"Isa.29.13","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Isa.29.13","value":"Isa.29.13"}],"t":"ref","v":"29:13"},"), proclaiming judgment and declaring that only a righteous remnant would survive (",{"href":"?bref=Isa.6.11-13","r":"Isa.6.11-13","rawR":"Isa.6.11-13","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Isa.6.11-13","value":"Isa.6.11-13"}],"t":"ref","v":"6:11-13"},"). He foretold the coming Messiah, the peaceful prince of God’s kingdom (",{"href":"?bref=Isa.9.6-7","r":"Isa.9.6-7","rawR":"Isa.9.6-7","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Isa.9.6-7","value":"Isa.9.6-7"}],"t":"ref","v":"9:6-7"},"; ",{"href":"?bref=Isa.11.1-11","r":"Isa.11.1-11","rawR":"Isa.11.1-11","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Isa.11.1-11","value":"Isa.11.1-11"}],"t":"ref","v":"11:1-11"},") who was also an obedient, suffering servant (",{"href":"?bref=Isa.53.3-12","r":"Isa.53.3-12","rawR":"Isa.53.3-12","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Isa.53.3-12","value":"Isa.53.3-12"}],"t":"ref","v":"53:3-12"},"). The book of Isaiah has more overtones of the New Testament than any other Old Testament book and is frequently cited by New Testament authors."],"sourceClass":"profile-body","t":"p"},{"c":["Passages for Further Study"],"sourceClass":"profile-refs-title","t":"h"},{"c":[{"href":"?bref=2Kgs.19.1-20.19","r":"2Kgs.19.1-20.19","rawR":"2Kgs.19.1-20.19","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"2Kgs.19.1-20.19","value":"2Kgs.19.1-20.19"}],"t":"ref","v":"2 Kgs 19:1–20:19"},"; ",{"href":"?bref=2Chr.26.22","r":"2Chr.26.22","rawR":"2Chr.26.22","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"2Chr.26.22","value":"2Chr.26.22"}],"t":"ref","v":"2 Chr 26:22"},"; ",{"href":"?bref=2Chr.32.20","r":"2Chr.32.20","rawR":"2Chr.32.20","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"2Chr.32.20","value":"2Chr.32.20"}],"t":"ref","v":"32:20"},", ",{"href":"?bref=2Chr.32.32","r":"2Chr.32.32","rawR":"2Chr.32.32","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"2Chr.32.32","value":"2Chr.32.32"}],"t":"ref","v":"32"},"; ",{"href":"?bref=Isa.1.1–66.24","r":"Isa.1.1–66.24","rawR":"Isa.1.1–66.24","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Isa.1.1–66.24","value":"Isa.1.1–66.24"}],"t":"ref","v":"Isa 1:1–66:24"},"; ",{"href":"?bref=Matt.3.3","r":"Matt.3.3","rawR":"Matt.3.3","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Matt.3.3","value":"Matt.3.3"}],"t":"ref","v":"Matt 3:3"},"; ",{"href":"?bref=Matt.4.14-16","r":"Matt.4.14-16","rawR":"Matt.4.14-16","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Matt.4.14-16","value":"Matt.4.14-16"}],"t":"ref","v":"4:14-16"},"; ",{"href":"?bref=Matt.8.17","r":"Matt.8.17","rawR":"Matt.8.17","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Matt.8.17","value":"Matt.8.17"}],"t":"ref","v":"8:17"},"; ",{"href":"?bref=Matt.12.17-21","r":"Matt.12.17-21","rawR":"Matt.12.17-21","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Matt.12.17-21","value":"Matt.12.17-21"}],"t":"ref","v":"12:17-21"},"; ",{"href":"?bref=Matt.13.14-15","r":"Matt.13.14-15","rawR":"Matt.13.14-15","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Matt.13.14-15","value":"Matt.13.14-15"}],"t":"ref","v":"13:14-15"},"; ",{"href":"?bref=Matt.15.7-9","r":"Matt.15.7-9","rawR":"Matt.15.7-9","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Matt.15.7-9","value":"Mat